# interplay_versions_resetup_reset_245_none: reset or stop under a live session, then the guest sets everything up again, at another version or at none. -reset tears the root down (FIFOs, tasks, displays) and writes version 0, EFI off and device-info max key 1; -stop only writes root page 0. What carries over is what the device holds outside the root: the version after stop, 0 after reset (so a guest that writes none gets version 0's features: 67 and 69 refused, 32 FIFOs), and the device-info max key the next blob uses. Tasks do not come back from the root page's task words (only a resume reads them): an exec on task 1 faults until it is defined again. The work before the teardown, after the new setup, and after a migration at the end gives the same bytes, by 67 or 55 as the version allows.
